1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,754
1991, Kuwait. Tanks speed across the barren desert.

2
00:00:04,755 --> 00:00:11,000
But how will this massive strike force get food, water and shelter?

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,028
1968, Vietnam. A wounded soldier is whisked to a nearby field hospital.

4
00:00:17,029 --> 00:00:20,000
Who cleared the jungle to build it?

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,720
1945, Normandy. How did a million soldiers get ashore?

6
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,360
Now, the heroic untold story of battlefield engineering on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:49,680
Because preparing for battle didn't usually have the drama of the battle itself.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,640
Most historians, novelists and movie makers focused on the fighting rather than the actions preceding the fighting.

9
00:00:57,520 --> 00:01:05,120
But history shows in the world of battlefield engineering, there was more than enough drama and fighting to go around.

10
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:16,260
It has been the job of the battlefield engineer to lay the groundwork quite literally for the battle to come.

11
00:01:16,261 --> 00:01:23,260
They built roads, railroads and bridges.

12
00:01:23,261 --> 00:01:28,060
They constructed airfields, barracks, hospitals, supply depots,

13
00:01:28,061 --> 00:01:33,520
and all the other facilities that supported combat operations.

14
00:01:33,521 --> 00:01:36,320
They were earthmovers and river crossers.

15
00:01:36,321 --> 00:01:43,720
They built things and they destroyed things.

16
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:49,120
Their mission was critical because without bases from which to operate, there was no way to mount an attack.

17
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:56,040
Without open supply lines, an army would quickly run out of food, water, and ammunition.

18
00:01:57,560 --> 00:01:59,980
The Navy's engineers, the Seabees, have a slogan.

19
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:03,820
We build. We fight.

20
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:11,620
When I'm asked what the Seabees do, the short answer is we do anything and everything, and we do it quickly.

21
00:02:11,621 --> 00:02:16,540
Ask an army engineer and the answer will sound much the same.

22
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:18,900
Remember one thing.

23
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,920
The engineers are the ones who get them in and the ones who get them out.

24
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:27,340
And if you don't believe me, just ask an infantryman. They'll tell you.

25
00:02:32,160 --> 00:02:34,940
Throughout history, man has built fortifications.

26
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:38,380
Building those fortifications and breaking through them

27
00:02:38,381 --> 00:02:41,600
give rise to the first battlefield engineers.

28
00:02:41,620 --> 00:02:45,560
There's a dichotomy between warriors and soldiers.

29
00:02:45,561 --> 00:02:47,440
Warriors are interested in defeating individuals.

30
00:02:47,441 --> 00:02:51,800
They're not interested in conquering a province, defeating an entire army.

31
00:02:51,801 --> 00:02:53,680
They don't care what's going on next to them.

32
00:02:53,681 --> 00:02:56,300
They care about the one person they're fighting.

33
00:02:56,301 --> 00:03:00,100
A soldier is part of a unit, which is part of a greater unit, which is part of an army.

34
00:03:00,101 --> 00:03:02,700
He fights as a member of a collective.

35
00:03:02,701 --> 00:03:04,900
He has greater goals.

36
00:03:04,901 --> 00:03:07,580
And this is perhaps the most profound difference.

37
00:03:07,581 --> 00:03:11,282
engineering flows out of soldiers as opposed to warriors.

38
00:03:11,283 --> 00:03:13,620
Warriors are not military engineers.

39
00:03:14,740 --> 00:03:15,772
Soldiers are.

40
00:03:15,773 --> 00:03:22,600
It is strangely ironic that the same engineers who are called upon to devise a defense

41
00:03:22,601 --> 00:03:26,960
are often later called upon to devise a way to break through it.

42
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:30,954
The fort begets the battering ram.

43
00:03:30,955 --> 00:03:35,000
The more heavily fortified castle begets the siege engine

44
00:03:35,001 --> 00:03:36,236
and the catapult.

45
00:03:36,237 --> 00:03:41,760
As defenses strengthened, they require bigger, stronger, more lethal ways to

46
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:43,076
penetrate them.

47
00:03:43,077 --> 00:03:50,360
Over 2,000 years ago, the Romans decided that if they were going to rule the world,

48
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:52,426
they needed a way to get there.

49
00:03:52,427 --> 00:03:55,600
Some of their roads and bridges were so well constructed,

50
00:03:55,601 --> 00:03:57,320
they remain to this day.

51
00:03:57,321 --> 00:04:02,840
Following the fall of the Roman Empire and the Dark Ages and the Middle Ages,

52
00:04:02,841 --> 00:04:06,000
defenders built better and bigger walls,

53
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:09,460
causing attackers to go over or under them.

54
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:13,240
To counter the wall, to counter the defenses

55
00:04:13,241 --> 00:04:15,160
that the engineers had come up with then,

56
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:18,020
machines were created,

57
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:19,900
one of them being the catapult,

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,140
a way to get over the wall.

59
00:04:22,080 --> 00:04:25,020
You threw a rock, in some cases you threw a dead horse,

60
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:27,440
or you threw a dead body to spread disease.

61
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:32,020
Another way of dealing with walls

62
00:04:32,021 --> 00:04:33,100
was to breach them.

63
00:04:33,680 --> 00:04:36,980
This was usually done by soldiers called sappers and miners.

64
00:04:37,980 --> 00:04:40,320
Sappers would dig the trenches above the battlefield

65
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:42,440
and they would trench up to the enemy fortifications

66
00:04:42,441 --> 00:04:46,200
and at the last minute, a group of volunteers known as the Forlorn Hope

67
00:04:46,201 --> 00:04:50,680
would attack the fortification walls and destroy them to breach to the soft underbelly inside.

68
00:04:51,380 --> 00:04:53,540
Now the miners would tunnel underneath the fortifications

69
00:04:53,541 --> 00:04:56,320
to collapse the enemy's walls and breach from the underneath side.

70
00:04:56,321 --> 00:05:04,780
Castles were having mines dug under their walls with such frequency that there arose a need for mine detectors.

71
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:08,100
Mine detection can go back as far as the Middle Ages,

72
00:05:08,360 --> 00:05:13,160
when women would carry big copper bowls filled with water down to the basement of the castle or the fortification,

73
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:19,980
set it on the stone floor, and be domestic down there, sewing, cooking, but keeping an eye on the water.

74
00:05:19,981 --> 00:05:23,840
And when they'd see ripples developing in the surface of the water, they'd say,

75
00:05:23,841 --> 00:05:24,712
And I detect a mine.

76
00:05:24,713 --> 00:05:30,160
I detect the vibrations of the picks and the shovels of the miners that are undermining our foundation to collapse our walls.

77
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:37,140
As fortifications became virtually impenetrable, sieges became less effective.

78
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:45,720
But then in the 14th and 15th centuries, the introduction of gunpowder and cannons changed battlefields forever.

79
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:53,740
If you have a castle and you could keep your opponent in the field for months, your opponent would end up starving.

80
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:54,663
outside your walls.

81
00:05:54,664 --> 00:05:57,520
Winter would come, the opponent would not be able to provision his

82
00:05:57,521 --> 00:05:58,711
armies, and you won.

83
00:05:58,712 --> 00:06:03,060
When gunpowder came along, the opponent knocks down your wall, he marches

84
00:06:03,061 --> 00:06:06,860
by you, and he wins. Essentially, gunpowder made the castle obsolete.

85
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:13,860
Following the death of the castle, the innovative French military engineer,

86
00:06:14,020 --> 00:06:19,175
Sébastien Vaubat, proposed a radically new type of fortification.

87
00:06:19,176 --> 00:06:20,920
Thick walls low to the

88
00:06:20,921 --> 00:06:23,863
ground helped protect from cannon fire.

89
00:06:23,864 --> 00:06:27,560
A star-shaped design allowed defenders inside the

90
00:06:27,561 --> 00:06:31,718
fort to fire at attackers from virtually any angle.

91
00:06:31,719 --> 00:06:35,060
By the time of the American Revolutionary

92
00:06:35,061 --> 00:06:40,100
War, the monumental changes in battlefield tactics caused by the use of gunpowder and

93
00:06:40,101 --> 00:06:44,294
cannons had completely changed how wars were fought.

94
00:06:44,295 --> 00:06:47,520
An army had to have trained and educated

95
00:06:47,521 --> 00:06:48,980
battlefield engineers.

96
00:06:49,700 --> 00:06:51,000
In the American Revolution,

97
00:06:51,380 --> 00:06:52,360
the army was created

98
00:06:52,361 --> 00:06:53,560
all dressed up to go somewhere

99
00:06:53,561 --> 00:06:54,960
off the fight and found out,

100
00:06:55,460 --> 00:06:56,540
gee, guys, we're missing something.

101
00:06:56,620 --> 00:06:57,540
We have no engineers.

102
00:06:59,300 --> 00:07:01,080
Congress says, let us have some.

103
00:07:01,160 --> 00:07:02,380
Let us promote some people

104
00:07:02,381 --> 00:07:03,140
to be engineers.

105
00:07:03,980 --> 00:07:04,760
No, you don't understand.

106
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:05,900
We have no engineers.

107
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:07,600
We have to hire them.

108
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:11,200
In 1775, George Washington

109
00:07:11,201 --> 00:07:12,680
created what was to become

110
00:07:12,681 --> 00:07:14,560
the Army Corps of Engineers.

111
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:16,680
And it was fortuitous

112
00:07:16,681 --> 00:07:20,940
that the French, perhaps the best engineers in the world at the time, were more than happy

113
00:07:20,941 --> 00:07:23,560
to supply engineers to help defeat the English.

114
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:32,820
After the War of 1802, Thomas Jefferson created the nation's first engineering school at a

115
00:07:32,821 --> 00:07:35,065
fort located at West Point, New York.

116
00:07:35,066 --> 00:07:38,220
It would be the nation's primary source of engineers

117
00:07:38,221 --> 00:07:41,420
and military officers for decades to come.

118
00:07:41,421 --> 00:07:52,100
in 1861 the american civil war introduced the biggest revolution in battlefield engineering

119
00:07:52,101 --> 00:07:59,320
since gunpowder railroads the ability to quickly transport large numbers of troops

120
00:07:59,321 --> 00:08:03,600
to wherever they were needed changed military tactics dramatically

121
00:08:03,601 --> 00:08:13,040
the northern armies controlled 30 000 miles of america's railroads more track than could be found

122
00:08:13,041 --> 00:08:18,960
in the rest of the world and they continued laying even more rail even as they ripped up

123
00:08:18,961 --> 00:08:25,520
hundreds of miles of confederate track the union army in the north had the industrial ability to

124
00:08:25,521 --> 00:08:31,280
manufacture locomotives and extend rail lines at a prodigious rate while the confederate army in the

125
00:08:31,281 --> 00:08:36,480
south lacked that ability railroads were being destroyed and locomotives burned much faster than

126
00:08:36,481 --> 00:08:41,040
they could be replaced in the south creating a tremendous disadvantage for southern mobility

127
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:48,960
railroad bridges were built and rebuilt again and again during this war this one was rebuilt seven

128
00:08:48,961 --> 00:08:55,680
times when abraham lincoln saw this bridge over the potomac he said it looked like it had been

129
00:08:55,681 --> 00:08:58,517
made from bean poles and corn stalks.

130
00:08:58,518 --> 00:09:03,040
This bridge over the Chattahoochee River was 800 feet long,

131
00:09:03,780 --> 00:09:08,561
nearly 100 feet high. It took over four and a half days to build.

132
00:09:08,562 --> 00:09:10,620
An astounding accomplishment

133
00:09:10,621 --> 00:09:12,528
in that or any time period.

134
00:09:12,529 --> 00:09:16,840
And timber bridges weren't the only record-breaking projects.

135
00:09:18,580 --> 00:09:23,580
The most significant military engineering development in the Civil War was the advent

136
00:09:23,581 --> 00:09:25,640
of the ponton or pontoon bridge.

137
00:09:25,980 --> 00:09:28,340
Bridges built across boats or ships

138
00:09:28,341 --> 00:09:31,060
date as early as the 5th century B.C.

139
00:09:31,660 --> 00:09:33,200
But for us, it was a new challenge.

140
00:09:34,420 --> 00:09:36,140
Engineers responded to that challenge

141
00:09:36,141 --> 00:09:38,320
to the point that in 1864,

142
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:41,780
they built a 2,200-foot float bridge

143
00:09:41,781 --> 00:09:43,300
across the James River

144
00:09:43,301 --> 00:09:45,060
in slightly more than 10 hours.

145
00:09:45,580 --> 00:09:47,720
That's the longest American float bridge

146
00:09:47,721 --> 00:09:50,240
built by the engineers in American history.

147
00:09:50,241 --> 00:09:55,360
The success and rapid capture of old style forts

148
00:09:55,361 --> 00:09:58,520
signal the beginning of the end of fixed masonry forts.

149
00:09:59,380 --> 00:10:02,440
They were replaced by massive earthwork fortifications.

150
00:10:03,280 --> 00:10:07,440
In the future, trenches would replace walls as the dominant type of defense.

151
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:13,640
The widespread use of mortars and cannons on railroad cars

152
00:10:13,641 --> 00:10:16,940
heralded a new more mobile use of heavy artillery.

153
00:10:16,941 --> 00:10:25,940
On a smaller scale but still ominous note, another innovation appeared that would drastically alter ground warfare.

154
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:36,740
In 1862, we have a Confederate engineer officer who will put a fuse on an artillery shell and bury that shell in a road.

155
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:41,040
When the Union cavalry comes along, the shell detonates.

156
00:10:41,480 --> 00:10:46,700
That's the first use in the American Army of what we would refer to today as a landmine.

157
00:10:46,940 --> 00:10:53,940
The battlefield innovations witnessed during the Civil War provided a glimpse into the future.

158
00:10:53,941 --> 00:11:03,940
A half century later, a war they would call the Great War would continue a trend toward a more entrenched, more horrific kind of combat.

159
00:11:06,940 --> 00:11:11,940
Without battlefield engineers in the American colonies, the British troops had no camps or living quarters.

160
00:11:11,941 --> 00:11:15,940
Instead, they were installed in colonists' private homes.

161
00:11:15,941 --> 00:11:18,820
This was one of the grievances leading to the American Revolution.

162
00:11:19,460 --> 00:11:22,520
Battlefield Engineering will continue on Modern Marvels.

163
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:26,760
You're watching Battlefield Engineering on Modern Marvels.

164
00:11:28,980 --> 00:11:32,880
World War I was called the war to end all wars.

165
00:11:34,340 --> 00:11:39,240
But it might better have been called the war to begin all modern wars.

166
00:11:45,940 --> 00:11:52,940
When the fighting began in Europe in the summer of 1914, advances in the technology of killing

167
00:11:52,941 --> 00:11:56,940
took more soldiers' lives in less time than ever before.

168
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:06,940
The world had never seen anything like the trenches of World War I, a far cry from the ditches of previous conflicts.

169
00:12:06,941 --> 00:12:09,940
They intertwined for hundreds of miles.

170
00:12:09,941 --> 00:12:19,540
Front line trenches, communications trenches, support trenches, all were tied together in city-like networks by battlefield engineers.

171
00:12:21,740 --> 00:12:30,220
At the beginning of the war, generals schooled in classic battlefield techniques were still accustomed to sending vast waves of troops at each other.

172
00:12:30,221 --> 00:12:43,235
Because of advances in weaponry and battlefield engineering, these tactics of the past led to human slaughter of almost unimaginable proportions, especially when

173
00:12:43,236 --> 00:12:45,660
troops were ordered to attack.

174
00:12:45,661 --> 00:12:51,920
During the Battle of Verdun, one of the bloodiest battles in the history of the world,

175
00:12:51,921 --> 00:13:00,240
it is estimated that 400,000 French soldiers and 600,000 Germans lost their lives in just six months.

176
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:07,540
French warfare had to be the worst situation when one had to eventually go over the top.

177
00:13:08,100 --> 00:13:12,760
And in that case, no man's land must have seemed like an eternity to reach the other side.

178
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:14,940
Many people didn't reach the other side.

179
00:13:14,941 --> 00:13:18,120
You had landmines. You had barbed wire.

180
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:22,020
Barbed wire was there not only to slow you down, but to literally ensnare you,

181
00:13:22,480 --> 00:13:26,560
to allow the grim reaper of the First World War to do its grisly work,

182
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:29,320
the machine gun, to take out entire units.

183
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:33,020
With miles of barbed wire to get across,

184
00:13:33,160 --> 00:13:36,020
battlefield engineers needed the newly introduced tank

185
00:13:36,021 --> 00:13:39,380
to break through the barriers and roll over obstacles.

186
00:13:39,381 --> 00:13:48,380
The tank led to the development of the anti-tank mine, which in turn led to a need to breach minefields.

187
00:13:52,380 --> 00:13:57,380
Although the full impact of military aircraft would not be felt until World War II,

188
00:13:57,381 --> 00:14:02,380
their introduction here created a whole new set of problems and challenges.

189
00:14:02,381 --> 00:14:09,380
Targets far behind the front lines, which were previously safe, were now vulnerable to attack.

190
00:14:09,381 --> 00:14:16,380
And an army's position and movements were open to surveillance from the air.

191
00:14:16,381 --> 00:14:23,380
Since the days of the Greeks and Romans, wars were won by the army that could move the fastest.

192
00:14:23,381 --> 00:14:30,380
Motorized warfare took fighting to a new level of speed and conferred on battlefield engineers

193
00:14:30,381 --> 00:14:33,380
engineers a heightened level of importance.

194
00:14:33,381 --> 00:14:39,780
World War I was also important to the engineers in that it is the point in which the engineers

195
00:14:39,781 --> 00:14:43,600
began their love affair with the internal combustion engine.

196
00:14:43,601 --> 00:14:51,900
It marks the beginning of the engineers shift from horsepower and manpower to machine power.

197
00:14:51,901 --> 00:14:54,200
That evolution has continued to today.

198
00:14:54,201 --> 00:15:00,020
Without the machinery of today, the engineers job would be virtually impossible.

199
00:15:00,021 --> 00:15:04,480
that were built to handle horse-drawn carts had to be improved to handle the faster, heavier

200
00:15:04,481 --> 00:15:09,841
gasoline-powered vehicles. Bridges had to be stronger.

201
00:15:09,842 --> 00:15:12,820
Rail lines had to be repaired.

202
00:15:13,740 --> 00:15:18,720
To a large degree, without engineers in the battlefield, no one could move.

203
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:27,160
When the United States entered the war in 1917, the first request from the British and the French

204
00:15:27,161 --> 00:15:30,960
governments was for engineers.

205
00:15:30,961 --> 00:15:37,680
Newly organized railway regiments and combat engineers rushed to France.

206
00:15:37,681 --> 00:15:44,420
Not surprisingly, the first US casualties were engineers.

207
00:15:44,421 --> 00:15:48,480
This was the war that formalized the concept of fighting engineers.

208
00:15:48,481 --> 00:15:53,260
Prior to this, engineering divisions were separate from combat units.

209
00:15:53,261 --> 00:16:01,260
they were expected to carry shovels and rifles, hammers and hand grenades.

210
00:16:01,261 --> 00:16:07,260
When American engineers began to arrive in big numbers, their effect was immediate.

211
00:16:07,261 --> 00:16:11,260
The Germans would blow up the bridges, but the Americans would rebuild them.

212
00:16:11,261 --> 00:16:13,260
They'd blow up the switches and the Americans would rebuild them.

213
00:16:13,261 --> 00:16:19,260
They couldn't successfully blow up every foot of track, so we'd use what was left.

214
00:16:19,261 --> 00:16:29,680
Behind the lines, the engineers' efforts of building ports, roads, railroads, and support facilities produced some staggering statistics.

215
00:16:31,420 --> 00:16:33,669
The 20th Engineers, the U.S.

216
00:16:33,670 --> 00:16:41,780
Army's largest regiment, helped produce 200 million board feet of lumber and 4 million railroad ties.

217
00:16:41,781 --> 00:16:50,080
The scope of the American war effort, which only lasted a couple of years, was truly amazing.

218
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:54,120
We brought two million men to France and we had to feed them.

219
00:16:54,340 --> 00:17:00,400
And the engineers were responsible for making sure that we had fresh American food for these soldiers.

220
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:10,760
To satisfy a tremendous need for food refrigeration, one ice-making plant produced 500 tons of ice per day.

221
00:17:10,761 --> 00:17:16,040
Another refrigeration plant could store over 5,000 tons of beef.

222
00:17:17,480 --> 00:17:22,840
American engineers constructed a bakery that baked 4,000 tons of bread a day.

223
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:29,200
But the most telling statistic of the war involved people, not war supplies.

224
00:17:30,500 --> 00:17:34,300
Experts say 14 million soldiers and civilians may have died.

225
00:17:34,301 --> 00:17:42,660
It would now be up to battlefield engineers to figure out ways to defend against an even

226
00:17:42,661 --> 00:17:54,060
more terrifying war machine that would soon roll through Europe.

227
00:17:54,061 --> 00:17:58,600
Following World War I, the leaders of France decided to protect the country by constructing

228
00:17:58,601 --> 00:18:01,500
an elaborate system of defense.

229
00:18:01,501 --> 00:18:07,340
The Maginot Line was a series of fortifications that stretched 200 miles along the eastern French border.

230
00:18:08,340 --> 00:18:15,780
It consisted of gun emplacements, barracks, and underground command posts, all connected by railroad tunnels.

231
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:20,660
But as in the past, a new defense spawned a new offense.

232
00:18:21,580 --> 00:18:31,100
The heavily mechanized blitzkrieg outran the French and in swiftly storming through Belgium went around the heavily fortified Maginot Line.

233
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:38,500
Once again, the army that could move faster was the prevailing force.

234
00:18:38,501 --> 00:18:44,500
Newly designed airplanes were equipped with much more powerful machine guns and bombs.

235
00:18:44,501 --> 00:18:49,500
Vastly improved tanks were faster and armed with more killing power.

236
00:18:53,500 --> 00:18:59,500
All of this new technology triggered the stimulus response mechanism of battlefield engineering.

237
00:18:59,501 --> 00:19:05,540
bombers needed airfields airfields in turn were bombed and needed to be rebuilt

238
00:19:05,541 --> 00:19:11,740
bridges were blown up and needed to be replaced or repaired in response

239
00:19:11,741 --> 00:19:16,900
engineers created prefabricated foot bridges treadway bridges and dummy

240
00:19:16,901 --> 00:19:23,280
bridges as decoys general Douglas MacArthur who was himself a top of his

241
00:19:23,281 --> 00:19:28,300
class engineer graduate of West Point described World War two as an

242
00:19:28,301 --> 00:19:34,700
engineers war. You're watching Battlefield Engineering on Modern Models.

243
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:48,480
The attack on Pearl Harbor in 1941 caused the United States Navy to change the way it built

244
00:19:48,481 --> 00:19:49,932
its bases overseas.

245
00:19:49,933 --> 00:19:55,740
Until the surprise strike, the Navy had used civilian engineers and workers.

246
00:19:55,741 --> 00:20:03,740
But shortly after Pearl Harbor on Wake Island, 2300 miles southwest of Hawaii, some civilian

247
00:20:03,741 --> 00:20:06,740
workers were captured by the Japanese.

248
00:20:06,741 --> 00:20:13,700
Under the Geneva Convention, these civilians, if they take up arms, are not protected by

249
00:20:13,701 --> 00:20:17,740
any of the covenants of war and can be executed as spies.

250
00:20:17,741 --> 00:20:20,740
And in a few cases, this actually happened.

251
00:20:20,741 --> 00:20:26,600
Admiral Ben Morell got the go-ahead to immediately create a naval construction battalion made up of

252
00:20:26,601 --> 00:20:28,242
engineers trained to fight.

253
00:20:28,243 --> 00:20:32,500
The name Seabees was coined from Seabee, the initials for construction

254
00:20:32,501 --> 00:20:33,258
battalions.

255
00:20:33,259 --> 00:20:38,700
Their mascot was a fighting bee carrying a machine gun, a wrench, and a hammer.

256
00:20:40,840 --> 00:20:50,460
Mostly our troop, our gang, were from construction companies that the Navy went through

257
00:20:50,461 --> 00:20:56,200
and literally just bought out and uniformed up and sent out.

258
00:20:57,660 --> 00:21:02,180
As a result of looking for engineers and tradesmen with some experience,

259
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:05,840
the average CB in World War II was in his 30s.

260
00:21:06,400 --> 00:21:11,940
Many had wives and children and were exempt from the draft but volunteered anyway.

261
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:16,600
The much younger Marines had a standing joke.

262
00:21:17,260 --> 00:21:20,440
Be kind to a CB. He might be your father.

263
00:21:20,460 --> 00:21:27,600
These old men in their 30s were the guys who were going to save your bacon and do extraordinary things for you.

264
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:35,200
Building an airstrip while having to fight off the enemy at the same time often created heroic challenges.

265
00:21:36,200 --> 00:21:42,900
Usually a CB would put down his tools and fight, but Aurelio Tatsona decided to use his bulldozer as a weapon.

266
00:21:42,901 --> 00:21:52,248
The Japanese were in a pillbox, they were holding up an advance on an island, and Aurelio Tatsoni drove his bulldozer up and buried the pillbox, pushed dirt over it,

267
00:21:52,249 --> 00:21:59,840
and simply buried it along with the occupants, all except for a dog, a Japanese mascot that survived and was adopted by the battalion.

268
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:05,840
Another example of CB heroics involves Captain Wilfred Painter.

269
00:22:05,841 --> 00:22:11,160
Captain Painter would come ashore in a rubber boat, face blacked out, all the rest, Tommy gun, very dramatic,

270
00:22:11,960 --> 00:22:15,420
lay out the U.S. bases on the island while the Japanese are still there.

271
00:22:15,920 --> 00:22:17,220
After he did that, he would leave.

272
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:21,520
Then the Marines would come and take the island, and the Seabuse would immediately go ashore and build the bases.

273
00:22:22,980 --> 00:22:30,240
But the most famous example of battlefield engineer heroics must be D-Day, the invasion of Normandy.

274
00:22:35,840 --> 00:22:38,480
Long before the actual invasion of Normandy,

275
00:22:38,640 --> 00:22:42,320
Allied forces were involved in a massive buildup of men and materiel.

276
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:46,820
The Germans had fortified the French coast with artillery,

277
00:22:47,100 --> 00:22:51,320
cement bunkers, barbed wire, and over a million mines.

278
00:22:52,760 --> 00:22:57,260
The mines posed a serious challenge to the troops who would have to get through them.

279
00:22:57,261 --> 00:23:05,620
As a commander, I felt very strongly that it was necessary to train people to work,

280
00:23:05,621 --> 00:23:07,460
in a dangerous situation.

281
00:23:09,580 --> 00:23:12,600
Creating that pressure-filled situation in advance

282
00:23:12,601 --> 00:23:14,940
involved using live booby-trap mines.

283
00:23:16,280 --> 00:23:18,780
One soldier lost his life in the training.

284
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:21,820
Other soldiers cracked under the strain

285
00:23:21,821 --> 00:23:23,560
of finding and diffusing mines

286
00:23:23,561 --> 00:23:25,460
that could blow up at any moment.

287
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:31,740
When we got through,

288
00:23:31,741 --> 00:23:34,880
when it came to the question in France

289
00:23:34,881 --> 00:23:38,560
of removing the mines, we knew who were capable of doing it.

290
00:23:38,960 --> 00:23:43,680
And those people were used, and people who we found were not able to

291
00:23:43,681 --> 00:23:45,440
were given other missions.

292
00:23:47,660 --> 00:23:50,680
In addition to heavily fortifying the coastline,

293
00:23:50,681 --> 00:23:52,600
the Germans held all the ports.

294
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:55,860
An invasion without a harbor to land supplies

295
00:23:55,861 --> 00:23:58,800
would have been not only foolhardy, but suicidal.

296
00:23:58,801 --> 00:24:08,540
The audacious solution of the Allied battlefield engineers was both highly complex and daringly simple.

297
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:17,480
It was called Operation Mulberry, and it involved building a harbor in pieces and floating it across the English Channel.

298
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:26,580
Those pieces would be moved using a versatile system of construction that used five foot by seven foot metal boxes.

299
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:32,460
What these were was the tried and true pontoon.

300
00:24:33,060 --> 00:24:39,080
In this case, put together, seven long, three together tied here with an outboard engine,

301
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:41,160
and now it becomes a ferry.

302
00:24:41,860 --> 00:24:47,560
A flat bed that can carry troops, men, materiel, heavy equipment ashore.

303
00:24:48,020 --> 00:24:52,520
You take the same configuration apart with the engine off and latch it together in a long form,

304
00:24:52,521 --> 00:24:55,240
and you have a causeway or a pier.

305
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:08,800
On June 6, 1944, the largest amphibious force ever assembled landed on the beach at Normandy.

306
00:25:12,800 --> 00:25:20,800
Four to five thousand ships, eleven thousand planes, and on the first day, one hundred fifty-five thousand troops came ashore.

307
00:25:20,801 --> 00:25:21,501
ashore.

308
00:25:21,502 --> 00:25:28,320
Immediately before this massive assault, the combat engineers specially trained to clear

309
00:25:28,321 --> 00:25:34,357
mines snuck ashore in the dark to clear the way for these troops.

310
00:25:34,358 --> 00:25:36,400
Throughout the attack,

311
00:25:36,401 --> 00:25:41,316
the engineers were in the most vulnerable positions.

312
00:25:41,317 --> 00:25:44,720
One of the units lost 67% in wounded

313
00:25:44,721 --> 00:25:46,623
or killed. It's pretty hard to do it.

314
00:25:46,624 --> 00:25:49,400
Sometimes it would have been easier to pick up a rifle

315
00:25:49,401 --> 00:25:54,100
or a machine gun and fire back than to do your job of clearing the way.

316
00:25:55,020 --> 00:25:57,340
Even as troops were still coming ashore,

317
00:25:57,920 --> 00:26:01,080
engineers began the construction of the Mulberry Harbor.

318
00:26:01,940 --> 00:26:05,240
The Mulberry system was a system of prefabricated dock facilities

319
00:26:05,241 --> 00:26:08,920
that were made of cast concrete filled with air and floated across the channel.

320
00:26:08,921 --> 00:26:13,240
And they were sunk right on the Normandy coast to build our own prefabricated docks.

321
00:26:14,660 --> 00:26:18,380
The first phase involved dynamiting a number of old ships

322
00:26:18,381 --> 00:26:21,540
and then sinking a long line of cement caissons.

323
00:26:23,840 --> 00:26:28,180
Together, the ships and the caissons would form offshore breakwaters.

324
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:34,920
Next, a floating roadway was built stretching from the shore

325
00:26:34,921 --> 00:26:38,040
out to the protected area behind the breakwater.

326
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:44,540
Once a wharf was constructed at the end of that road,

327
00:26:44,541 --> 00:26:50,860
It allowed the unloading of tanks, trucks, men, and supplies from larger ships.

328
00:26:51,940 --> 00:26:54,840
In less than two weeks, the harbor was fully functional.

329
00:26:56,020 --> 00:26:59,680
By the end of the month, approximately one million men came ashore.

330
00:27:01,540 --> 00:27:04,980
In addition to building the artificial harbors at Normandy,

331
00:27:05,380 --> 00:27:08,800
the engineers operated the beach camps, which housed the troops.

332
00:27:09,460 --> 00:27:13,820
They also laid fuel and water lines and ferried back and forth across the channel

333
00:27:13,821 --> 00:27:15,500
with more men and supplies.

334
00:27:16,620 --> 00:27:20,160
Without the engineers accomplishing all of these tasks successfully,

335
00:27:20,600 --> 00:27:23,960
there could have been no Normandy invasion, no D-Day.

336
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:29,980
Once Allied troops were on the European mainland,

337
00:27:30,560 --> 00:27:35,160
they faced the difficult task of getting fast-moving tanks across rivers and streams.

338
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:38,820
British engineers developed the Bailey Bridge,

339
00:27:39,200 --> 00:27:43,680
a portable structure with no single piece that weighed more than 600 pounds.

340
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:48,820
It was used when there wasn't enough time to build a traditional bridge.

341
00:27:48,821 --> 00:27:55,820
In the infantry, you have the term, which means, well, let's get going, let's get the job done.

342
00:27:55,821 --> 00:28:02,820
In the engineers, we have the term, which represents picking up Bailey bridge panels for construction projects.

343
00:28:02,821 --> 00:28:05,820
Lay hold, heave the bridge panel.

344
00:28:05,821 --> 00:28:08,820
You have soldiers working in unison to build a ten-ton bridge.

345
00:28:08,821 --> 00:28:13,240
bridge, it's choreographed like a ballet. Everyone has to work together and lift and

346
00:28:13,241 --> 00:28:17,402
heave together. It talks about the physical work of the engineers.

347
00:28:17,403 --> 00:28:18,600
The ability to work

348
00:28:18,601 --> 00:28:23,700
together to come up with practical solutions in the field is the common link between the

349
00:28:23,701 --> 00:28:27,592
battlefield engineers of the past 2,500 years.

350
00:28:27,593 --> 00:28:31,400
But as the weapons of war became deadlier and

351
00:28:31,401 --> 00:28:36,540
more powerful, the challenge facing the engineers became increasingly more difficult.

352
00:28:36,541 --> 00:28:43,540
You are watching Battlefield Engineering on Modern Models.

353
00:28:43,541 --> 00:28:52,540
In 1950, when the Korean War broke out, battlefield engineers were faced with a demolition job,

354
00:28:52,541 --> 00:28:57,540
breaking through fortifications, breaching minefields and blowing up bases.

355
00:28:57,541 --> 00:29:06,280
They were also charged with building vital airfields, bridges and roads.

356
00:29:07,160 --> 00:29:14,320
In addition, they cleared the jungle for mobile army surgical hospitals, better known as MASH units.

357
00:29:15,700 --> 00:29:19,900
Korea changed dramatically the history of fatalities,

358
00:29:19,901 --> 00:29:27,720
because before Korea, 90% of all the wounded that were hit in the head or the belly died.

359
00:29:28,460 --> 00:29:34,940
And once the MASH were set up, 90% lived because they could get back to the MASH hospitals.

360
00:29:36,320 --> 00:29:42,180
One of the biggest challenges facing the engineers was the lack of raw materials to get the job done.

361
00:29:42,181 --> 00:29:44,940
Cement was not available.

362
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:47,240
Steel was not available.

363
00:29:48,100 --> 00:29:50,240
Timber was not available.

364
00:29:50,740 --> 00:29:56,360
You must remember that everything south, roughly everything south of the 38th parallel,

365
00:29:56,920 --> 00:29:59,720
was agriculturally inclined.

366
00:29:59,860 --> 00:30:00,640
Rice patties.

367
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:04,200
So it was very innovative engineering.

368
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:07,780
You couldn't do things precisely.

369
00:30:07,781 --> 00:30:14,780
An example of that innovative engineering involved figuring out how to get heavy machinery

370
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:18,000
to operate in sub-zero temperatures.

371
00:30:18,001 --> 00:30:21,960
One company kept their bulldozers warm in warming huts

372
00:30:21,961 --> 00:30:25,500
and then borrowed another idea from the ski slopes.

373
00:30:25,501 --> 00:30:32,500
The problem was once the bulldozer got warm, the blades got hot and the frozen earth would

374
00:30:32,501 --> 00:30:36,980
the frozen earth would stick to the bulldozer blade.

375
00:30:37,800 --> 00:30:41,820
So what we did was to radio over to Tokyo

376
00:30:41,821 --> 00:30:45,520
and had several thousand pounds of ski wax

377
00:30:45,521 --> 00:30:49,160
that dropped into our perimeter there,

378
00:30:49,280 --> 00:30:51,320
and we used ski wax on the blades,

379
00:30:51,800 --> 00:30:54,560
and this enabled the blades to work

380
00:30:54,561 --> 00:30:57,420
without the earth freezing onto the blades.

381
00:30:57,421 --> 00:31:07,320
in korea because the front line was constantly shifting one day's advance was often the next

382
00:31:07,321 --> 00:31:13,460
day's retreat ironically sometimes engineers have to destroy what they just built

383
00:31:13,461 --> 00:31:21,920
one engineer battalion in korea built a thousand foot timber bridge for tank and logistical resupply

384
00:31:21,921 --> 00:31:26,420
and a week later had to pour diesel and gasoline on it and burn it.

385
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:30,640
The bridge was ultimately referred to as the Broken Heart Bridge,

386
00:31:30,700 --> 00:31:33,840
and the battalion was referred to later as the Broken Heart Battalion.

387
00:31:36,040 --> 00:31:39,640
Despite valiant individual efforts and innovative engineering,

388
00:31:40,180 --> 00:31:44,700
Broken Hearts were the order of the day for many of the soldiers in the Korean conflict.

389
00:31:45,560 --> 00:31:49,440
And that would also prove to be true of the United States' next big war.

390
00:31:51,920 --> 00:32:03,220
The engineering challenges posed by Vietnam was that, unlike other big wars of the 20th century, there was no front line.

391
00:32:03,920 --> 00:32:08,940
This was compounded by the fact that much of the war was being fought in a dense jungle.

392
00:32:09,940 --> 00:32:20,700
Since this was the first air mobility war, a primary responsibility of the battlefield engineer was to build and maintain airstrips and helicopter landing bases.

393
00:32:21,920 --> 00:32:29,260
Sometimes the engineers, to get into difficult areas, jungle areas, built-up areas, they had to helicopter in.

394
00:32:29,700 --> 00:32:33,640
There they would come down the rope ladders with their hand tools,

395
00:32:34,780 --> 00:32:39,040
clear an area for a bigger helicopter to come in, which would be carrying a bulldozer.

396
00:32:39,041 --> 00:32:44,040
That type of a base could be built by a company of engineers in about one day.

397
00:32:44,041 --> 00:32:54,640
The almost non-existent infrastructure in Vietnam meant that engineers were basically starting from scratch,

398
00:32:54,840 --> 00:33:02,080
having to build roads, drainage, and bridges, and having to do it with almost none of the raw materials required.

399
00:33:02,700 --> 00:33:04,780
What they did have was plenty of mud.

400
00:33:05,980 --> 00:33:11,480
Among our earliest taskings was the job of getting the Marines up out of the mud

401
00:33:11,481 --> 00:33:13,920
so that they could have decent living accommodations

402
00:33:13,921 --> 00:33:15,460
when they were not out in the field

403
00:33:15,461 --> 00:33:18,200
searching for the Viet Cong and the North Vietnamese.

404
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:21,800
The way we got them out of the mud

405
00:33:21,801 --> 00:33:25,080
was to build what's known as a Southeast Asia hut

406
00:33:25,081 --> 00:33:26,500
for everyone who was there.

407
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:29,620
We could put in 15, 20, 25 of these an hour

408
00:33:29,621 --> 00:33:32,080
once we got the prefab technique down.

409
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:36,560
The use of pontoon bridges wasn't really practical

410
00:33:36,561 --> 00:33:40,340
because the rivers were being used for day-to-day navigation.

411
00:33:40,341 --> 00:33:46,340
Instead, conventional cement and steel and timber bridges had to be constructed.

412
00:33:46,341 --> 00:33:53,340
To counter the overall lack of dockage space, engineers came up with the Dulong Pier.

413
00:33:53,341 --> 00:33:56,340
It came out of Charleston, South Carolina.

414
00:33:56,341 --> 00:34:01,340
It was a barge that was about 300 feet long by 90 feet wide,

415
00:34:01,341 --> 00:34:08,340
built in Charleston, towed across the water to Vietnam and then set in place.

416
00:34:08,341 --> 00:34:15,340
A team of 15 engineers could put it into position in about 45 days.

417
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:22,340
To build a timber pier this size would take six months.

418
00:34:25,340 --> 00:34:31,340
Another innovation was the instant airstrip, made possible by the use of T17 nylon membrane.

419
00:34:31,341 --> 00:34:33,340
membrane.

420
00:34:33,341 --> 00:34:38,380
Lighter and easier to transport than metal runway sections, it was glued together over

421
00:34:38,381 --> 00:34:42,640
a graded and compacted airstrip.

422
00:34:42,641 --> 00:34:51,860
The synthetic material created a surface so strong it could be used by huge aircraft.

423
00:34:51,861 --> 00:34:57,300
The introduction of the Rome Plough, a rugged bulldozer blade made in Rome, Georgia, was

424
00:34:57,301 --> 00:35:04,620
a tremendous asset and aided in the difficult task of clearing a jungle filled with mines.

425
00:35:04,621 --> 00:35:09,820
Although the Rome Plough could detonate some types of mines without being destroyed itself,

426
00:35:09,821 --> 00:35:15,160
most mines still had to be cleared by engineers on foot or in vehicles equipped with mine

427
00:35:15,161 --> 00:35:16,840
detectors.

428
00:35:16,841 --> 00:35:22,420
It was actually really scary to a certain extent.

429
00:35:22,421 --> 00:35:24,300
You didn't know what was going to happen.

430
00:35:24,301 --> 00:35:28,880
The mine detectors were good and they were bad in the sense that when you're going down

431
00:35:28,881 --> 00:35:33,600
a dirt road in a combat zone, there's so much shrapnel, there's just all kinds of metal

432
00:35:33,601 --> 00:35:39,760
in the road, so you really had to develop a sense of signal detection to determine whether

433
00:35:39,761 --> 00:35:45,420
it was a mine or something, and you never knew for sure what you were going to find.

434
00:35:47,700 --> 00:35:53,120
In addition to the mines, American engineers had to concern themselves with the tunnels

435
00:35:53,121 --> 00:35:54,460
built by the Viet Cong,

436
00:35:55,380 --> 00:35:57,200
engineering marvels in themselves.

437
00:35:58,380 --> 00:36:00,600
In Coo Chi province, near Saigon,

438
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:03,940
it was estimated that there were 200 miles of tunnels.

439
00:36:05,200 --> 00:36:07,560
Although U.S. forces tried several techniques

440
00:36:07,561 --> 00:36:08,560
to eliminate them,

441
00:36:09,020 --> 00:36:10,600
none was completely successful.

442
00:36:12,540 --> 00:36:14,940
The Viet Cong tunnel system in some areas

443
00:36:14,941 --> 00:36:15,860
was quite extensive.

444
00:36:16,700 --> 00:36:20,260
In some areas, it was even two to three layers deep.

445
00:36:20,261 --> 00:36:24,400
They could put hospital units down there.

446
00:36:25,400 --> 00:36:29,340
They could put maintenance and repair units down into the tunnels.

447
00:36:30,860 --> 00:36:32,900
And they were quite extensive and quite effective.

448
00:36:35,640 --> 00:36:41,320
Whether the United States troops would get the cooperation of the local people was often a question mark.

449
00:36:42,100 --> 00:36:45,260
But engineers had something extra going for them.

450
00:36:45,261 --> 00:36:52,160
The CB's mission, in addition to supporting the combat troops, was also to assist the

451
00:36:52,161 --> 00:36:55,000
local Vietnamese populace.

452
00:36:55,001 --> 00:36:58,811
And so the roads that we built not only supported the U.S.

453
00:36:58,812 --> 00:37:00,980
forces, they also were frequently

454
00:37:00,981 --> 00:37:06,580
of use to the Vietnamese or the North Vietnamese forces, and they supported the local economy.

455
00:37:06,581 --> 00:37:15,160
Therefore, we were able to go places and send single trucks that were marked very clearly

456
00:37:15,161 --> 00:37:20,200
with sea bees on them in areas that no one else would dare go.

457
00:37:22,560 --> 00:37:26,660
In a war in which few good things are said about the outcome,

458
00:37:27,280 --> 00:37:30,460
the construction projects of the American battlefield engineers

459
00:37:30,461 --> 00:37:34,100
may be the most positive lasting legacy of the conflict.

460
00:37:36,440 --> 00:37:39,880
The lessons learned in the jungles of Southeast Asia

461
00:37:39,881 --> 00:37:44,840
would some 20 years later be applied in a vastly different arena.

462
00:37:45,160 --> 00:37:48,640
the deserts of the Middle East.

463
00:37:49,120 --> 00:37:53,060
You're watching Battlefield Engineering on Modern Models.

464
00:37:55,640 --> 00:37:59,320
When U.S. troops started the buildup in Saudi Arabia,

465
00:37:59,800 --> 00:38:02,280
the challenge posed by a desert environment

466
00:38:02,281 --> 00:38:05,820
was poles apart from the muddy jungles of Vietnam.

467
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:11,320
You have mass formations of armored vehicles

468
00:38:11,321 --> 00:38:14,000
attacking fixed fortifications.

469
00:38:14,001 --> 00:38:17,140
something out of World War II, almost.

470
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:19,580
Vietnam was a low-intensity war.

471
00:38:20,080 --> 00:38:21,320
It was a brush fire war.

472
00:38:22,100 --> 00:38:25,160
You had armored vehicles there, not in mass,

473
00:38:25,640 --> 00:38:27,760
going up against fixed enemy positions,

474
00:38:27,900 --> 00:38:29,200
such as we found in the desert.

475
00:38:30,880 --> 00:38:35,220
The desert, as any soldier who has ever fought in it will tell you,

476
00:38:35,400 --> 00:38:36,860
is an enemy in itself.

477
00:38:37,680 --> 00:38:38,620
The sand was everywhere.

478
00:38:38,760 --> 00:38:40,320
It was kind of like dust storm days.

479
00:38:40,500 --> 00:38:41,740
The tents would be full of sand.

480
00:38:41,741 --> 00:38:47,580
your gear was, the equipment was, weapons had to be cleaned frequently if they would operate.

481
00:38:48,260 --> 00:38:50,440
The challenges were extreme.

482
00:38:53,580 --> 00:38:56,820
One of the extreme challenges was landmines.

483
00:38:57,920 --> 00:39:01,980
This time a new technique was employed to deal with the old problem.

484
00:39:03,680 --> 00:39:07,280
Engineers used a Miklik, or mine clearing, line charge,

485
00:39:07,281 --> 00:39:11,680
a device that fires a hose filled with plastic explosives.

486
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:18,560
After being launched across a minefield, it was detonated,

487
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:24,340
creating a safe passageway through the mines.

488
00:39:25,420 --> 00:39:29,940
After months of constructing millions of square feet of aircraft aprons,

489
00:39:30,420 --> 00:39:32,640
camps for tens of thousands of Marines,

490
00:39:33,180 --> 00:39:36,380
and hundreds of acres of ammunition and supply points,

491
00:39:36,381 --> 00:39:41,280
the engineers prepared to support the ground assault into Kuwait.

492
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:49,680
The most formidable task facing the battlefield engineers

493
00:39:49,681 --> 00:39:54,500
was the road network required by General Schwarzkopf's end-run attack.

494
00:39:55,500 --> 00:39:58,400
It spanned more than 30 miles of desert

495
00:39:58,401 --> 00:40:01,320
and required more than 200 miles of roads.

496
00:40:01,900 --> 00:40:05,560
Some were barely drivable until they were hurriedly improved

497
00:40:05,561 --> 00:40:07,000
just before the attack.

498
00:40:08,160 --> 00:40:11,680
Bottom line is, this road was, you know, at the beginning,

499
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:13,120
maybe you could go five miles an hour.

500
00:40:13,220 --> 00:40:15,840
Towards the end, you could drive 50, 55 miles an hour on it.

501
00:40:18,540 --> 00:40:20,400
Frankly, when you look at Desert Storm

502
00:40:20,401 --> 00:40:22,300
and try to apply the lessons to the next war,

503
00:40:22,700 --> 00:40:25,480
you've got to be careful because we're probably never going to find

504
00:40:25,481 --> 00:40:26,440
that infrastructure again,

505
00:40:26,860 --> 00:40:28,600
and we're probably never going to find an enemy again

506
00:40:28,601 --> 00:40:30,360
who says, I will sit here and wait for you

507
00:40:30,361 --> 00:40:32,320
to build up this amazing combat power.

508
00:40:35,560 --> 00:40:43,040
The future of battlefield engineering is as difficult to predict as tomorrow's headlines.

509
00:40:43,740 --> 00:40:44,515
But the U.S.

510
00:40:44,516 --> 00:40:50,840
Army is now preparing for adversaries who might use low-tech devices to combat high-tech weaponry.

511
00:40:51,920 --> 00:40:57,940
To counter this, engineers are developing ways to deal with what they call an asymmetric threat.

512
00:40:59,140 --> 00:41:05,360
A mine or an obstacle is a classic asymmetric threat because the enemy can put it in and then leave.

513
00:41:05,560 --> 00:41:10,180
He doesn't have any of his forces at risk, but when we get there, it slows us down,

514
00:41:10,620 --> 00:41:12,720
it takes us out of our plan, or it kills us.

515
00:41:14,400 --> 00:41:18,080
To counter this threat, the Army is developing new strategies.

516
00:41:19,080 --> 00:41:24,080
What we're trying to do in the future is to replace men with machinery, with mechanization,

517
00:41:25,040 --> 00:41:27,820
put fewer soldiers at risk, and do it faster.

518
00:41:28,720 --> 00:41:33,840
One of the weapon systems of the future which exists today is the M1 Breacher,

519
00:41:33,841 --> 00:41:39,480
better known as the Grizzly. It is equipped with mine detecting radar and

520
00:41:39,481 --> 00:41:45,360
designed to break through almost any kind of obstacle. Its most visible

521
00:41:45,361 --> 00:41:49,880
feature is a mine clearing blade that can maintain a steady depth of 15 inches

522
00:41:49,881 --> 00:41:55,080
as it plows a 14-foot wide path through minefields.

523
00:41:55,081 --> 00:42:00,780
The Grizzly is our replacement for the what we today call the orchestrated ballet

524
00:42:00,781 --> 00:42:02,240
of farm implements.

525
00:42:02,241 --> 00:42:04,080
And what we're trying to do with the Grizzly

526
00:42:04,081 --> 00:42:07,580
is get a tank platform that can keep up with our forces

527
00:42:07,581 --> 00:42:10,580
and is survivable, that can go in and replace all those

528
00:42:10,581 --> 00:42:12,520
soldiers and pieces of equipment.

529
00:42:12,521 --> 00:42:15,360
It makes the job for the commander on the battlefield

530
00:42:15,361 --> 00:42:17,860
much easier, and it maintains the tempo,

531
00:42:17,861 --> 00:42:20,740
because all he has to do is decide where he wants

532
00:42:20,741 --> 00:42:24,120
to do the breach, slow down the enemy's rate of fire,

533
00:42:24,121 --> 00:42:27,040
and tell the Grizzly operators, create a lane for me.

534
00:42:27,041 --> 00:42:34,960
The Wolverine consists of a computer-controlled aluminum bridge on an Abrams tank chassis.

535
00:42:35,900 --> 00:42:39,600
The 75-foot bridge only takes four minutes to deploy,

536
00:42:40,140 --> 00:42:43,320
and it can be picked up in less than 10 minutes for use elsewhere.

537
00:42:44,780 --> 00:42:46,740
An M1 tank weighs about 70 tons.

538
00:42:47,580 --> 00:42:49,400
There are a lot of bridges we can't put it over,

539
00:42:49,860 --> 00:42:53,040
and there are a lot of bridges that our enemies are going to blow up in front of us

540
00:42:53,041 --> 00:42:54,840
to keep us from crossing.

541
00:42:54,841 --> 00:42:58,220
So we need a way to rapidly bridge small gaps.

542
00:42:58,680 --> 00:43:02,180
And the Wolverine gives that to us in a platform that can keep up with the tank.

543
00:43:05,600 --> 00:43:12,780
To cross much larger gaps of up to 120 feet, engineers will use this quickly deployed bridge.

544
00:43:13,100 --> 00:43:19,860
With the heavy dry support bridge, we're going to take the mission that would take a company of 100 or more soldiers all day to build.

545
00:43:19,861 --> 00:43:24,900
and we're going to build that same bridge in about an hour and a half with 14 people.

546
00:43:27,880 --> 00:43:32,360
It is now the task of battlefield engineers to do more with less.

547
00:43:32,960 --> 00:43:37,060
But while their numbers may diminish, their importance seems to grow.

548
00:43:38,140 --> 00:43:42,460
Without the engineer, quite often the bombers would not fly.

549
00:43:43,160 --> 00:43:46,200
The tanks wouldn't cross the river and the infantry wouldn't advance.

550
00:43:46,201 --> 00:43:50,020
So the mission 2,000 years ago was the same as it was in Desert Storm.

551
00:43:50,021 --> 00:43:54,840
We built roads through the desert, we built bunkers and barracks facilities, and we brought

552
00:43:54,841 --> 00:43:56,120
water to our troops.

553
00:43:56,121 --> 00:43:58,220
So the mission hasn't changed.

554
00:43:58,221 --> 00:44:02,320
The only thing that's changed has been the technology.

555
00:44:02,321 --> 00:44:07,740
In the final analysis, despite the downsizing of the military, the fact remains that someone

556
00:44:07,741 --> 00:44:12,840
will be needed to operate the machines being used to replace the troops.

557
00:44:12,841 --> 00:44:18,020
If history is any guide, those operators will be battlefield engineers.

